David Damerell  <damerell@chiark.greenend.org.uk> wrote:
>I would be inclined to say, then, that 20-30K is fast; 50K average; and
>above 80K is slow. This would suggest that a midgame clock could start to
>make its presence felt around the 40K mark - a fast player can avoid it
>altogether, but would only start to mete out serious pain around 80-100K,
>to either demand a finish or grind the player down to nothing. By this
>time you hit 150K, anything goes.

However, another data point here is that Ellora had killed Baalzebub after
150,000 turns. Irritatingly, that is the only serious data we have about
unspoiled play.

Now of course the game is going to be inhumanly hard for unspoiled players
anyway, and it seems likely that Ellora would have died around then
anyway; but I think it's clear that a midgame clock has to have
perceptible effects. No-one needs to be told that you can starve by
wasting time in the early game; some messages would need to indicate
difficulty rampups in the middle game.
